=== plain-80: width 80, height 24, profile notty, dark, glyphs ascii, plain
# ssh refusals -------------------------------------------------$
$
# Note$
# Mock: The refusals before asking, each from the real reading:$
# the item, the host.$
$
# root, root-session -------------------------------------------$
$
# Warning$
# You're logged in as root over SSH. Turning root login off$
# would close the way you came in. Log in as an admin and run$
# hardn through sudo.$
$
# root, nopam-locked -------------------------------------------$
$
# Warning$
# No admin could log in over SSH afterwards: george's account is$
# locked (!) and sshd has no PAM. Fix that first.$
$
# root, match-allows -------------------------------------------$
$
# Warning$
# Match Address 192.0.2.0/24, in 20-site.conf, lets more in for$
# some connections, and hardn's setting wouldn't change that.$
# Change it there first.$
$
# root, not-read -----------------------------------------------$
$
# Warning$
# /run/sshd isn't there yet, so sshd -T can't run. Open one SSH$
# connection, then try again.$
$
# root, legacy-main --------------------------------------------$
$
# Warning$
# hardn's old SSH files are still here (hardn.conf). Move them$
# into 00-hardn.conf first: item 4.$
$
# root, include-other ------------------------------------------$
$
# Warning$
# sshd_config doesn't start with "Include$
# /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/*.conf", so hardn's settings wouldn't$
# be read first. Put that line first, then try again.$
$
# root, confirm-window -----------------------------------------$
$
# Warning$
# A change is waiting for hardn confirm. Confirm it from a new$
# connection, or let it roll back, then try again.$
$
# password, password-login -------------------------------------$
$
# Warning$
# alice would lose SSH login: alice has a password and no key.$
# Add a key in User Management first.$
$
# password, password-session -----------------------------------$
$
# Warning$
# You logged in with a password. Turning passwords off would$
# close the way you came in. Log in with a key first.$
$
# port, pve9 ---------------------------------------------------$
$
# Warning$
# The SSH port stays 22 on Proxmox VE: the cluster and pve-$
# firewall use it.$
$
# port, ufw-restricted -----------------------------------------$
$
# Warning$
# ufw lets port 22 in from 192.0.2.0/24 alone. A new port would$
# be open to anyone. Move that rule to the new port by hand$
# first.$
$
# port, legacy-v032 --------------------------------------------$
$
# Warning$
# hardn's old SSH files are still here (manage.conf,$
# listen.conf). Move them into 00-hardn.conf first: item 4.$
$
# migrate, legacy-v032 -----------------------------------------$
$
# Warning$
# /etc/systemd/system/ssh.socket.d/listen.conf (hardn v0.3.x)$
# sets ssh.socket to port 2230, but it never took effect:$
# ssh.socket is configured for [[::]:22 0.0.0.0:22] (no daemon-$
# reload since it was written, D9). Migrating it now could not$
# be rolled back: the rollback's daemon-reload would move$
# ssh.socket to port 2230 alone, off the port this host is$
# reached on (P-39). First make sure the firewall lets port 2230$
# through: ssh.socket then listens on port 2230 alone. Then let$
# listen.conf take effect while this session stays open: run$
# `systemctl daemon-reload` and `systemctl restart ssh.socket`$
# (established SSH sessions survive the restart), check that a$
# new SSH connection to port 2230 works, and run hardn again. A$
# reboot also lets it take effect, but ends this session: reboot$
# only with console access, in case port 2230 is not reachable.$
$
$

